<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Floor seepage (P23 extended, #149): the water balance of a closed hollow,
      A_lake = runoff x rain x catchment / (evaporation - rain + S),
    had no loss but evaporation, so in a wet climate every closed hollow filled to its rim and spilled. S is the
    EFFECTIVE seepage through the wetted floor in mm/yr: what a lake bed actually lets through once its own fines have
    sealed it (colmation). It is NOT the saturated conductivity of the material - clean sand conducts metres a day,
    but a sand-floored pond holds water for years because its bed seals; real seepage losses from reservoirs and
    ponds on sand and gravel run a few hundred to a few thousand mm/yr, and about nil on clay (the owner's review of
    the round 2 design fixed the original K_sat reasoning, which was off by 1000). A floor stands a hollow below its
    spill level whenever evaporation - rain + S > 0; with S = 0 the balance is exactly the old one.

    Floor class per tile (FloorSeepageRules.ClassOf): river alluvium sorted by LL_GrainSize; else deep soil; else the
    rock beneath (carbonate -> karst, open joints -> jointed rock, else hardpan). The cap S_eff = S x clamp01(d /
    disconnectDepth) uses d, how far the water table lies below the hollow's floor: the floor's height above the
    drainage line its outlet spills into. A floor at or below that line is a window on the groundwater and loses
    nothing (deep hollows and riverside lowlands keep their ponds); a floor disconnectDepth or more above it drains
    under gravity at its full S (shallow upland hollows on sand or karst stand low or dry). A hollow with no outlet is
    the low point its groundwater flows to, so it loses nothing either. Deserts change little: their hollows are
    already evaporation-limited, and their basin floors are mostly fine sediment.

    Scale check: evaporation = max(150, 400 + 60 x temp) mm/yr (SurfaceWaterExtension), so a wet climate's rain
    surplus over evaporation is ~1000-1300 mm/yr: sand, gravel and karst floors beat it (hollows stand low or dry),
    clay, silt and jointed rock do not (ponds and full hollows) - as in real humid regions, where karst and outwash
    plains have no surface hollows and clay lowlands are full of them.
  -->
  <LivingLandscapes.Geology.FloorSeepageDef>
    <defName>LL_FloorSeepage</defName>
    <label>floor seepage</label>

    <!-- clay/hardpan: a sealed bed or massive unfractured rock, an aquitard; observed losses are well under
         10 mm/yr, and 0 keeps a clay-floored hollow filling to its rim exactly as today. -->
    <seepageClayMmPerYear>0</seepageClayMmPerYear>
    <!-- silt: fine alluvium and deep weathered soil colmate tight; pond losses through silt beds run tens of
         mm/yr, not enough to beat a wet climate. -->
    <seepageSiltMmPerYear>20</seepageSiltMmPerYear>
    <!-- jointed rock: open joints (RockStrata Site.joint) carry water, but occupy a few percent of the bed area,
         so averaged over the floor a few hundred mm/yr. -->
    <seepageJointedRockMmPerYear>600</seepageJointedRockMmPerYear>
    <!-- sand: unsealed sand would drain metres a year; a colmated sand lake bed loses a few hundred to a few
         thousand mm/yr (reservoir seepage losses on sand). -->
    <seepageSandMmPerYear>1500</seepageSandMmPerYear>
    <!-- gravel: flow through gravel flushes the fines out instead of sealing, so gravel beds stay open at the
         high end of the few-thousand range. -->
    <seepageGravelMmPerYear>8000</seepageGravelMmPerYear>
    <!-- karst: conduits dissolved in carbonate rock swallow sinkhole ponds whole - one that drains in hours to
         days is tens of metres a year spread over the floor. -->
    <seepageKarstMmPerYear>20000</seepageKarstMmPerYear>

    <!-- LL_GrainSize classes (overlay labels, Sediment.xml): under 0.25 fine (silt floor), to 0.7 sand, over it gravel. -->
    <grainSilt>0.25</grainSilt>
    <grainGravel>0.7</grainGravel>
    <!-- soil this deep (m, LL_SoilDepth; the overlay tops out near 2.4) is weathered ground, not bedrock: a silt floor. -->
    <soilSiltDepth>1</soilSiltDepth>
    <!-- RockStrata Site.joint at or above this counts as an open joint under the hollow. -->
    <jointOpen>0.5</jointOpen>
    <!-- Depth of the water table below the floor (m) at which the bed is disconnected and seeps its full S: a lake
         bed loses water by gravity once the water table drops below the bed and its capillary fringe, a few metres;
         above that the loss grows with the depth (the head across the bed). -->
    <disconnectDepth>3</disconnectDepth>

    <!-- carbonate rocks whose ground weathers to karst: limestone, and marble where the tile carries it. -->
    <karstRockDefNames>
      <li>Limestone</li>
      <li>Marble</li>
    </karstRockDefNames>
  </LivingLandscapes.Geology.FloorSeepageDef>

</Defs>
